The configuration option `auto_mode` should be the option that disables interactive ask-user behavior. `EvoScientistConfig.auto_mode` must default to `False` and be settable to `True`. Middleware construction in both the default agent path and CLI agent path should include `AskUserMiddleware` only when `enable_ask_user` is true and `auto_mode` is false. The legacy `auto_approve` flag should not by itself suppress `AskUserMiddleware`. For the `serve` CLI command, passing `--auto-approve` should only set the `auto_approve` override. Passing `--auto-mode` should set `auto_mode=True`, imply `auto_approve=True`, and disable ask-user by setting `enable_ask_user=False`.

Manual conversation compaction should be aware of the model context window. The compact command should resolve the model’s context window from common model attributes such as `profile["max_input_tokens"]`, `profile["context_window"]`, `max_input_tokens`, or equivalent metadata, with a stable fallback. It should calculate the current conversation’s context utilization percentage and avoid manual compaction when the conversation is below a 40% context-window threshold. No-op compact results should report “Manual compact not needed” and include token count, context window, percent used, and a clear reason such as `Conversation is below the manual compact threshold (X% < 40%).` When real LLM input token usage is available from the most recent `usage_metadata`, manual compact display values should use that input-token count so the compact result matches the status bar, while still basing internal message partitioning and savings calculations on message-level tokens.

`CompactResult` should carry enough information for the UI to display compaction status, including context window, context utilization percent, and the human-readable summary text produced by a successful manual compaction. Successful manual compaction should expose a Rich renderable/panel titled `Context Compacted` containing just the summary body. Summary text may arrive with wrappers such as `Here is a summary of the conversation to date:` or `<summary>...</summary>` and the UI should display only the actual summary content. If no summary is available, no compact summary renderable should be produced.

Streaming summarization events need a clearer lifecycle. When summarization output begins, the stream should emit a `summarization_start` event before `summarization` content. Summarization content may come either from normal message chunks tagged with `lc_source == "summarization"` or from `_summarization_event` data included in update payloads. Existing persisted summarization events present in the initial agent state should not be re-emitted as new summarization output. The stream state should track `is_summarizing`, set it on `summarization_start` and `summarization`, and clear it on normal assistant text, `done`, or `error`. `usage_stats` handling should be robust to missing, `None`, non-numeric, or negative values, should accumulate total input/output tokens, and should also remember the last positive input/output token counts as `last_input_tokens` and `last_output_tokens`.

The TUI should treat `summarization_start`, `summarization`, and `usage_stats` as part of the active summarization panel and should not finalize the panel for those event types. Other visible event types such as thinking, text, tool calls/results, tool selection, subagent events, ask-user/interrupt, done, and error should finalize an active summarization panel. Loading-style widgets should share timer behavior through a common timed status widget base. `LoadingWidget` should advance spinner frame and elapsed time on ticks and cleanly stop its timer during cleanup. A `CompactingWidget` should exist to represent an in-progress compact operation. A collapsible `CompactSummaryWidget` should exist for manual compact summaries, initialize collapsed, store its content, report a character-count label such as `5 chars`, and support toggling collapsed state.

Tool display formatting should better identify memory operations. `format_tool_compact` should treat file tool paths passed as either `path` or `file_path` as equivalent, and should label operations on `/memory`, `/memory/...`, or `MEMORY.md` as `Reading memory` for `read_file` and `Updating memory` for `write_file`/`edit_file`. A result-aware formatter should also infer memory operations when tool arguments are sparse: for write/edit results mentioning `/memory/` or `MEMORY.md`, display `Updating memory`; for read results containing the memory document marker such as `# EvoScientist Memory`, display `Reading memory`. The TUI tool-call header should use this inference so completed memory edits with empty args are still shown as `Updating memory`.
